answer.javascript:
**Root cause.** The parser buffers block-list items (`- package`) in `Reader#items` and only copies them onto the task in `closeList()`. A new setting or a new task header calls `closeList()` first, but `finish()`, which runs at the end of the file, only closes the task:

```javascript
finish() {
  this.closeTask();
  return this.tasks;
}
```

So when the **last task in a file ends with a block list**, its dependencies are silently dropped. `publish` became a root task: Kahn's queue starts it right after `fetch-deps`, and the critical-path pass stops at `ui-tests`. The web pipeline hides the bug because its block list (`e2e-tests`) is followed by another setting, and its last task uses an inline list.

**Fix.**

```javascript
finish() {
  this.closeList();
  this.closeTask();
  return this.tasks;
}
```

Equivalently, make `closeTask()` call `closeList()` itself so no caller can forget.

**Verify.** Log `parsePipeline(MOBILE_RELEASE).at(-1).deps` before and after, then run the phase 1 tests. A strong answer explains why the graph and scheduler code were innocent (they planned exactly the graph they were given) and adds a regression test with a block list at the very end of a file, ideally followed by a comment line.

